03:00:51.120 --> 03:00:51.620
you get into.

03:00:51.620 --> 03:00:56.620
This really weird ontological territory where the past and even Schrodinger himself would

03:00:56.620 --> 03:01:02.620
say a particle is measured in the present, you know, it might change its past and you

03:01:02.620 --> 03:01:05.440
get into retro causality in quantum mechanics.

03:01:05.440 --> 03:01:10.180
There's obviously spooky action at a distance, but there's also spooky action across time.

03:01:10.180 --> 03:01:17.080
It seems like, um, if you have two entangled photons and two double slit experiments run

03:01:17.080 --> 03:01:20.660
with those double, you know, entangled photons, it seems like photon B that you're doing the

03:01:20.660 --> 03:01:21.100
experiment with.

03:01:21.100 --> 03:01:24.800
With two days in the future, if you measure that and you don't measure the first one,

03:01:24.800 --> 03:01:28.820
the measurement of the one in the future is affecting the one of the measurement in the

03:01:28.820 --> 03:01:29.820
past.

03:01:29.820 --> 03:01:30.820
Exactly.

03:01:30.820 --> 03:01:31.820
There's a great book called biocentrism.

03:01:31.820 --> 03:01:34.720
That's this guy, Robert Lanza writes about this stuff.

03:01:34.720 --> 03:01:40.990
So you get into really trippy territory where the past becomes the present is this constricted

03:01:40.990 --> 03:01:46.170
present and, but it's like an hourglass where you have probable futures and probable pasts.

03:01:46.170 --> 03:01:47.170
Right.

03:01:47.170 --> 03:01:50.970
And the present is this measurement sensor that is single line of code.

03:01:50.970 --> 03:01:54.690
It's like updating the past and maybe updating the future at the same time.

03:01:54.690 --> 03:01:55.690
Right.

03:01:55.690 --> 03:02:00.490
And it's this very strange sort of model, but you, you, there's no real way to argue

03:02:00.490 --> 03:02:02.690
